<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gellone Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">804</span> · Also known as Abbey of Saint-Guilhem-le-Désert</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gellone Abbey is a Benedictine abbey founded in 804 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-gellone">William of Gellone</a>, count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, in a gorge of the Hérault valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a>. William retired there as a monk and died there, and his cult, with a relic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a>, made the abbey a stop on the pilgrimage road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>. The village around it took the name Saint-Guilhem-le-Désert.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gellone-abbey.jpg" alt="Gellone Abbey" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wikinicoj · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ext%C3%A9rieur_de_l%27Abbaye_de_Gellone_%C3%A0_Saint_guilhem_le_d%C3%A9sert.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
